format 1
adapter curriculum 1
unit: radiation-meets-matter

objective u3-penetration

statement:
    Know that alpha particles are stopped by paper or skin, beta particles by a thin layer of metal, and that gamma rays are the most penetrating of the three.

misconception: One kind of shield stops every kind of radiation.

item u3-l2-choice-alpha
type: choice
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-radiation-basics
references: ref-openstax-chem-21-6

prompt:
    What is enough to stop alpha particles?

options:
    {"id":"lead","label":"Several inches of lead","note":"That would stop them, but it is far more than they need. They are heavy and run out of energy very quickly."}
    {"id":"paper","label":"A sheet or two of paper"}
    {"id":"none","label":"Nothing can stop them","note":"Alpha particles are the easiest of the three to stop."}
    {"id":"wall","label":"A thick concrete wall","note":"That is far more than they need. Paper or skin is enough."}

answer: paper

explanation:
    An alpha particle is heavy, so it loses its energy over a very short distance. Paper, or the top layer of skin, is enough to stop it.

item u3-l2-choice-beta
type: choice
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-radiation-basics
references: ref-openstax-chem-21-6

prompt:
    What stops beta particles?

options:
    {"id":"paper","label":"A sheet of paper","note":"Beta particles are small and fast, and they pass through paper."}
    {"id":"lead","label":"Several inches of lead","note":"They do not need anything like that. A thin layer of metal stops them."}
    {"id":"none","label":"Nothing can stop them","note":"A thin layer of metal does."}
    {"id":"metal","label":"A thin layer of metal, such as aluminum"}

answer: metal

explanation:
    A beta particle is small and fast, so paper does not stop it. A thin layer of metal does.

item u3-l2-match-stops
type: match
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-radiation-basics
references: ref-openstax-chem-21-6

prompt:
    Match each kind of radiation to what stops it.

pairs:
    {"id":"alpha","left":"Alpha","right":"A sheet of paper"}
    {"id":"beta","left":"Beta","right":"A thin layer of metal"}
    {"id":"gamma","left":"Gamma","right":"Inches of lead or feet of concrete"}

explanation:
    Each step up needs something denser or thicker: paper for alpha, thin metal for beta, and several inches of lead or a few feet of concrete for gamma.

item u3-l2-order-pen
type: order
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-radiation-basics
references: ref-openstax-chem-21-6

prompt:
    Put them in order, from the easiest to stop to the hardest to stop.

items:
    {"id":"alpha","label":"Alpha particles (stopped by paper)"}
    {"id":"beta","label":"Beta particles (stopped by thin metal)"}
    {"id":"gamma","label":"Gamma rays (need thick lead or concrete)"}

explanation:
    Paper is enough for alpha, thin metal is needed for beta, and thick, dense material for gamma. The more it takes to stop it, the more penetrating the radiation is.

item u3-l2-predict-paper
type: predict
surfaces: lesson
references: ref-epa-radiation-basics

prompt:
    A sheet of paper is held in front of a stream of alpha particles. What do you think happens?

input: choice

options:
    {"id":"stops","label":"The paper stops them"}
    {"id":"through","label":"They go straight through"}
    {"id":"burn","label":"The paper burns"}
    {"id":"bounce","label":"They bounce back at full speed"}

answer: stops

explanation:
    Your guess is not scored. Keep it in mind while we look at alpha, beta and gamma.

item u3-l2-tom-skin
type: true_or_myth
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-radiation-basics

statement:
    The outer layer of your skin stops alpha particles.

answer: true

explanation:
    True. Alpha particles cannot get through the outer layer of skin, for the same reason a sheet of paper stops them: they are heavy and lose their energy fast.

item u3-x-most-penetrating
type: choice
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-radiation-basics

prompt:
    Which of these is the most penetrating?

options:
    {"id":"alpha","label":"Alpha particles","note":"Alpha particles are the least penetrating. Paper stops them."}
    {"id":"gamma","label":"Gamma rays"}
    {"id":"beta","label":"Beta particles","note":"Beta particles go farther than alpha, but a thin layer of metal stops them. Gamma goes farther still."}

answer: gamma

explanation:
    Gamma rays pass through skin and clothing and need thick, dense material to stop them. That makes them the most penetrating of the three.

item u3-x-skin-alpha
type: choice
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-radiation-basics

prompt:
    Why can alpha particles be stopped by the top layer of skin?

options:
    {"id":"heavy","label":"They are heavy and lose their energy over a very short distance"}
    {"id":"slow","label":"They are the fastest radiation, so they stop on contact","note":"Alpha particles are not fast. Being heavy is what makes them stop quickly."}
    {"id":"warm","label":"Skin is warm and absorbs radiation","note":"Warmth has nothing to do with it. It is about how heavy the particle is."}

answer: heavy

explanation:
    An alpha particle is heavy, so it uses up its energy over a very short distance. That is why paper or the outer layer of skin is enough.

item u3-x-tom-same-stop
type: true_or_myth
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-radiation-basics

statement:
    The same shield stops alpha, beta and gamma equally well.

answer: myth

explanation:
    Myth. They are stopped by very different things: paper for alpha, thin metal for beta, and thick, dense material for gamma. A shield has to suit the radiation.
